+# Historical SIP scanner implementation outline
+
+These are archival notes about the October 6 design discussion. They contain no implementation tasks or release instructions. Current release and authorship rules are in [CLAUDE.md](../../../CLAUDE.md) and [DECISIONS.md](../../implementation-plan/DECISIONS.md). The current acceptance mapping is [R00](../../delivery/plans/R00-cc-sip-scanner.json), the executable contract and operations are in the [runbook](../../runbooks/CC-SIP-SCANNER.md), and delivered changes are recorded in the [CHANGELOG](../../implementation-plan/CHANGELOG.md).
+
+The original outline used platform base `1e32fd9` and Command Center base `2f7bef3`. It described an internal paper-strategy data feed based on shared Schwab data and Redis, with isolated feature work and separate native installation. Those base revisions and proposed filenames are historical context, not current prerequisites.
+
+The work was grouped into calculation/calendar contracts, read-only provider and durable storage adapters, service/route/dispatcher integration, and the native Command Center consumer. The calculation discussion covered exact opening-window ratios, liquidity and ATR filters, deterministic ranking, stock classification and missing-data refusal. Storage discussion covered partial progress, immutable session results and owner-checked leases.
+
+The native consumer discussion covered authenticated reads, freshness checks, delayed publication, selection deadlines and continued management of existing positions. Verification discussion included negative fixtures, the actual paper signal/risk/fill/exit loop, endpoint and dispatcher checks, and separate native evidence. These topics describe the original work breakdown; executable tests and the current acceptance mapping carry the implemented coverage.
+
+The historical review focus included classification completeness, missing-window distinctions, lock overlap, holiday/DST boundaries and truthful pending/empty/unavailable outcomes. The outline did not establish a production deployment, live provider readiness, Windows installation or portfolio valuation.
