+# Historical Command Center SIP scanner design discussion
+
+These are archival notes about the October 6 design discussion. They contain no implementation tasks or release instructions. Current release and authorship rules are in [CLAUDE.md](../../../CLAUDE.md) and [DECISIONS.md](../../implementation-plan/DECISIONS.md). The current acceptance mapping is [R00](../../delivery/plans/R00-cc-sip-scanner.json), the executable contract and operations are in the [runbook](../../runbooks/CC-SIP-SCANNER.md), and delivered changes are recorded in the [CHANGELOG](../../implementation-plan/CHANGELOG.md).
+
+The discussion concerned the missing input for the existing paper Stocks-in-Play ORB strategy. Its proposed shape was a versioned, authenticated stock snapshot prepared before the open and ranked after the completed five-minute opening window. The configured platform universe supplied stock classifications; it was not described as every US stock. Trading signals, stops, sizing and execution mode were outside the integration scope.
+
+The proposed data path used shared Schwab market data, per-session Redis preparation progress, owner-checked work leases and an immutable publication. Historical windows supplied the relative-volume denominator; missing history, transient failures and valid empty results were distinct. Provider reads were separated from the authenticated machine endpoint, which served stored data.
+
+The consumer discussion included strict date/window/row checks, initial-selection freshness, bounded retries through the selection deadline and continued exit management during unavailable input. A fixed watchlist remained an explicitly configured alternative rather than an implicit replacement for failed relative-volume scanning.
+
+The original verification discussion covered filter/calculation boundaries, calendar transitions, credentials, storage/lease failures, repeated publication and the actual paper entry/exit loop. Native installation, deployed credentials and portfolio valuation were separate evidence at the time of the discussion. This archive does not describe their current completion status or grant operational authority; current behavior, limits and release evidence are documented in the linked sources.
